.TH FIND 1
.SH NAME
find \- find files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
pathname-list expression
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B find
recursively descends the directory hierarchy for each pathname in the
.I pathname-list
(i.e., one or more pathnames) seeking files that match a boolean
.I expression
written in the primaries given below.
In the descriptions, the argument
.I n
is used as a decimal integer where
.BI + n
means more than
.IR n ,
.BI \- n
means less than
.I n
and
.I n
means exactly
.IR n .
.TP 12
.BI \-name " filename"
True if the
.I filename
argument matches the current file name.
Normal Shell argument syntax may be used if escaped (watch out for `[', `?' and `*').
.TP
.BI \-perm " onum"
True if the file permission flags exactly match the octal number
.I onum
(see
.BR chmod (1)).
If
.I onum
is prefixed by a minus sign, more flag bits (017777, see
.BR stat (2))
become significant and the flags are compared:
.IR "(flags & onum)==onum" .
.TP
.BI \-type " c"
True if the type of the file is
.IR c ,
where
.I c
is
.BR b ,
.BR c ,
.B d
or
.B f
for block special file, character special file, directory or plain file.
.TP
.BI \-links " n"
True if the file has
.I n
links.
.TP
.BI \-user " uname"
True if the file belongs to the user
.I uname
(login name or numeric user ID).
.TP
.BI \-group " gname"
True if the file belongs to group
.I gname
(group name or numeric group ID).
.TP
.BI \-size " n"
True if the file is
.I n
blocks long (512 bytes per block).
.TP
.BI \-inum " n"
True if the file has inode number
.IR n .
.TP
.BI \-atime " n"
True if the file has been accessed in
.I n
days.
.TP
.BI \-mtime " n"
True if the file has been modified in
.I n
days.
.TP
.BI \-exec " command"
True if the executed command returns a zero value as exit status.
The end of the command must be punctuated by an escaped semicolon.
A command argument `{}' is replaced by the current pathname.
.TP
.BI \-ok " command"
Like
.B \-exec
except that the generated command is written on the standard output, then the standard input is read and the command executed only upon response
.BR y .
.TP
.B \-print
Always true; causes the current pathname (to the matched file) to be printed to the standard output.
If
.B \-print
is not given, file names will not be printed.
.TP
.BI \-newer " file"
True if the current file has been modified more recently than the argument
.IR file .
.PP
The primaries may be combined using the following operators (in order of decreasing precedence):
.TP 4
1)
A parenthesized group of primaries and operators (parentheses are special to the Shell and must be escaped).
.TP
2)
The negation of a primary (`!' is the unary
.B not
operator).
.TP
3)
Concatenation of primaries (the
.B and
operation is implied by the juxtaposition of two primaries).
.TP
4)
Alternation of primaries (`\-o' is the
.B or
operator).
.SH EXAMPLES
To find all files in directory
.I /u1
called
.IR animal :
.RS
.nf
find /u1 \-name animal \-print
.fi
.RE
To remove all files named
.I a.out
or
.I *.o
that have not been accessed for a week:
.RS
.nf
.sp
find / \e( \-name a.out \-o \-name '*.o' \e)\e
	\-atime +7 \-exec rm {} \e;
.fi
.RE
To find all files owned by guest or demo in
.IR /u0 :
.RS
.nf
find /u0 \e( \-user guest \-o \-user demo\e) \-print
.fi
.RE
Note the use of parentheses.
Without them, the
.B \-print
would apply only to
.B \-user
.IR demo ;
consequently, files owned by guest (\fB\-user\fR \fIguest\fR) would never be listed.
.SH FILES
.nf
/etc/passwd
/etc/group
/bin/pwd
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), test(1), filsys(4)
.SH BUGS
The syntax is painful.
.sp
